1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,323
Right now, by one scientist's estimate, your
brain could hold about a

2
00:00:04,323 --> 00:00:06,390
plastic spoon's worth of plastic.

3
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:11,651
That estimate comes from a 2025 study that
analysed brain tissue from people

4
00:00:11,651 --> 00:00:16,220
who had died, and reported far more tiny
plastic than anyone expected.

5
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:17,982
They found it in blood.

6
00:00:17,982 --> 00:00:22,558
In the arteries of patients who then had more
heart attacks and strokes.

7
00:00:22,558 --> 00:00:24,020
In the water you drink.

8
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:29,111
And the plastic industry is accused of
knowing, for decades, that recycling

9
00:00:29,111 --> 00:00:32,050
would never solve the problem. It denies that.

10
00:00:32,350 --> 00:00:33,726
But there's a twist.

11
00:00:33,726 --> 00:00:38,613
A growing number of scientists now say some of
these terrifying numbers

12
00:00:38,613 --> 00:00:41,710
might be wrong. And that could be even worse.

13
00:00:42,610 --> 00:00:47,560
To understand how plastic got inside you,
start with how much of it there is.

14
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:52,498
In twenty years, the world doubled its plastic
production, to around four

15
00:00:52,498 --> 00:00:55,040
hundred and sixty million tonnes a year.

16
00:00:55,340 --> 00:01:00,897
And only about nine percent of all that
plastic waste actually gets recycled.

17
00:01:00,897 --> 00:01:03,640
The rest is burned, buried, or dumped.

18
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:09,190
Here's the problem. Plastic doesn't really go
away. It just gets smaller.

19
00:01:09,490 --> 00:01:14,901
Sun, waves and heat break it into
microplastics, smaller than a grain of

20
00:01:14,901 --> 00:01:19,710
rice, and then into nanoplastics, far thinner
than a human hair.

21
00:01:20,010 --> 00:01:24,200
Small enough to slip into places plastic was
never supposed to go.

22
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:26,333
And it isn't only bottles.

23
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:30,915
Every time you drive, your tyres shed plastic
dust onto the road.

24
00:01:30,915 --> 00:01:34,510
Every time you wash a fleece, it sheds tiny
fibres.

25
00:01:34,810 --> 00:01:39,525
One 2017 estimate found that washing synthetic
clothes and wearing down

26
00:01:39,525 --> 00:01:44,505
tyres cause almost two thirds of the plastic
that reaches the ocean already

27
00:01:44,505 --> 00:01:45,700
as tiny particles.

28
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,960
In 2024, one study looked at bottled water
with a powerful new microscope

29
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:51,640
technique.

30
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:56,245
It found an average of about two hundred and
forty thousand plastic

31
00:01:56,245 --> 00:02:00,100
particles in a single litre. Most of them were
nanoplastics.

32
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,260
And it doesn't stay in the bottle.

33
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:08,780
In 2022, researchers in Amsterdam tested the
blood of twenty two healthy adults.

34
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:12,510
Seventeen of them had plastic in their blood.

35
00:02:12,810 --> 00:02:17,032
Then doctors in Italy looked inside the
clogged neck arteries of patients

36
00:02:17,032 --> 00:02:17,900
having surgery.

37
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:21,630
Most of the plaques had micro or nanoplastics
in them.

38
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:26,203
And over the next three years, those patients
were about four and a half

39
00:02:26,203 --> 00:02:29,950
times more likely to have a heart attack, a
stroke, or die.

40
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:33,432
The study couldn't prove the plastic caused
it.

41
00:02:33,432 --> 00:02:36,410
But it set off alarm bells around the world.

42
00:02:36,710 --> 00:02:41,645
It's even turned up in the placenta, the organ
that feeds a baby before it's

43
00:02:41,645 --> 00:02:41,970
born.

44
00:02:41,970 --> 00:02:46,840
A small Italian study found plastic in four of
the six placentas it tested.

45
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:48,930
And then, the brain.

46
00:02:49,230 --> 00:02:54,892
The 2025 study compared brains from people who
died in 2016 with brains from

47
00:02:54,892 --> 00:02:56,680
people who died in 2024.

48
00:02:56,980 --> 00:03:00,728
The newer brains held about fifty percent more
plastic.

49
00:03:00,728 --> 00:03:03,658
Brains had far more than livers or kidneys.

50
00:03:03,658 --> 00:03:07,270
And the brains of people with dementia had
even more.

51
00:03:07,570 --> 00:03:10,841
Again, that doesn't prove plastic causes
dementia.

52
00:03:10,841 --> 00:03:14,570
A damaged brain might simply be worse at
clearing it out.

53
00:03:14,870 --> 00:03:20,292
But if plastic is everywhere, inside everyone,
somebody had to let it get

54
00:03:20,292 --> 00:03:20,960
this far.

55
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:27,790
For decades, you were told the answer was
simple. Rinse it. Sort it. Recycle it.

56
00:03:28,090 --> 00:03:33,204
An NPR investigation found industry documents
from as far back as the 1970s

57
00:03:33,204 --> 00:03:37,840
doubting that plastic recycling could ever pay
off on a large scale.

58
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:41,590
The industry says it never intentionally
misled anyone.

59
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:46,867
In 2024, California's attorney general sued
ExxonMobil, one of the world's

60
00:03:46,867 --> 00:03:51,440
biggest plastic producers, accusing it of
deceiving the public about

61
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:52,920
recycling for decades.

62
00:03:53,220 --> 00:03:58,740
Exxon denies it, and says its advanced
recycling works. The case isn't over.

63
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:01,365
And production isn't slowing down.

64
00:04:01,365 --> 00:04:06,493
Without new rules, the OECD projects the world
could use almost three times

65
00:04:06,493 --> 00:04:11,280
as much plastic by 2060, with less than a
fifth of the waste recycled.

66
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:16,850
But just as public fear reached its peak, the
science itself started to crack.

67
00:04:17,750 --> 00:04:20,730
Here's how many of these studies measured
plastic.

68
00:04:20,730 --> 00:04:24,962
They heat a tissue sample until it breaks
apart, then read the chemical

69
00:04:24,962 --> 00:04:26,750
fragments, like a fingerprint.

70
00:04:27,050 --> 00:04:27,852
The problem?

71
00:04:27,852 --> 00:04:33,000
Some fragments from ordinary body fat look a
lot like fragments from plastic.

72
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:37,060
Take out the water, and the brain is about
sixty percent fat.

73
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:41,870
In other words, some of that plastic… might
not be plastic at all.

74
00:04:42,170 --> 00:04:46,922
One analysis listed eighteen studies that
didn't properly account for these

75
00:04:46,922 --> 00:04:47,936
false positives.

76
00:04:47,936 --> 00:04:52,688
Scientists have now formally challenged at
least seven high-profile papers,

77
00:04:52,688 --> 00:04:57,250
including the brain study, the artery study
and the bottled water study.

78
00:04:57,550 --> 00:05:02,175
Some critics even suggest that rising obesity,
more fat in the body, could

79
00:05:02,175 --> 00:05:04,800
explain part of the jump in brain plastic.

80
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:07,154
The original researchers push back.

81
00:05:07,154 --> 00:05:11,555
They say the critics are speculating without
data of their own, though they

82
00:05:11,555 --> 00:05:14,020
admit their methods can still be improved.

83
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:18,902
One of the scientists behind the blood study
says she's still convinced the

84
00:05:18,902 --> 00:05:19,941
plastic is there.

85
00:05:19,941 --> 00:05:24,340
But the true amount could be half what they
measured, or ten times more.

86
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:29,209
Critics say quite a few papers, in the rush to
publish, skipped basic lab

87
00:05:29,209 --> 00:05:32,840
checks, like running blank samples to catch
contamination.

88
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:38,090
So is the whole microplastics scare just hype?
Not so fast.

89
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:43,836
Even one of the harshest critics says it's
safe to assume plastic is inside us.

90
00:05:43,836 --> 00:05:46,780
The fight is over how much, and what it's
doing.

91
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:51,880
And in October, a study in Nature found that
tiny plastic in water isn't as

92
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:53,352
harmless as we thought.

93
00:05:53,352 --> 00:05:57,640
Its surface can produce aggressive molecules
that can damage cells.

94
00:05:57,940 --> 00:06:00,178
That was in the lab, not in people.

95
00:06:00,178 --> 00:06:04,590
But it's a possible way plastic could do real
damage inside the body.

96
00:06:04,890 --> 00:06:07,209
And plastic rarely travels alone.

97
00:06:07,209 --> 00:06:12,057
Many plastics contain added chemicals, like
BPA and phthalates, which

98
00:06:12,057 --> 00:06:15,360
pediatricians warn can interfere with
hormones.

99
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:17,694
Governments are starting to move.

100
00:06:17,694 --> 00:06:22,254
The EPA has, for the first time, proposed
adding microplastics to its list

101
00:06:22,254 --> 00:06:26,445
of drinking water contaminants it may one day
regulate. And the U.S.

102
00:06:26,445 --> 00:06:30,944
health department is spending a hundred and
forty four million dollars to

103
00:06:30,944 --> 00:06:31,930
study the risks.

104
00:06:32,230 --> 00:06:36,533
Meanwhile, some clinics already charge
thousands to clean plastic out of

105
00:06:36,533 --> 00:06:39,940
your blood, before anyone knows how much is
really there.

106
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:44,488
And the global treaty meant to end plastic
pollution is still stuck, with

107
00:06:44,488 --> 00:06:47,340
countries split over whether to limit
production.

108
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:50,980
Which leaves you in the strangest possible
place.

109
00:06:51,880 --> 00:06:56,311
The scary headlines may have overstated how
much plastic is in your brain.

110
00:06:56,311 --> 00:07:00,442
And shaky studies could even help the plastic
industry wave the whole

111
00:07:00,442 --> 00:07:01,220
problem away.

112
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:06,408
But plastic is almost certainly inside you,
it's in the water you drink, and

113
00:07:06,408 --> 00:07:08,080
production keeps climbing.

114
00:07:08,380 --> 00:07:13,041
Pediatricians already suggest a few cheap
precautions: don't microwave food

115
00:07:13,041 --> 00:07:17,578
in plastic when you can avoid it, and keep
plastic out of the dishwasher.

116
00:07:17,578 --> 00:07:19,380
Heat makes it shed chemicals.

117
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:22,871
The real question isn't whether there's
plastic in you.

118
00:07:22,871 --> 00:07:27,280
It's how long we keep making more of it,
before we find out what it's doing.

119
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:31,430
So be honest: are you checking your water
bottle right now?
